        // Data source: «Техника кино и телевидения» 1989 No.4 and 1990 No.1 -- PDF/PROFILES/SOVIET/Техника_кино_и_телевидения_1989.pdf and ..._1990.pdf, 84 pages each, ABBYY FineReader 15 OCR over letterpress scans. ADDED 2026-08-24, owner-reported. ⚠ READ THE PROVENANCE CLASS BEFORE THE NUMBERS: these are Soviet technical periodicals, so method rule 14 applies -- but they are NOT independent of each other and only one of them is second-hand. The 1990 article (journal pp 56-59, PDF pp 58-61) is a TRANSLATION OF FUJI'S OWN PAPER, authored Kozo Noguchi, Yukihide Urata, Koichi Murai and prepared for the journal by A. V. Redko; the 1989 article (journal pp 68-70, PDF pp 70-72) is a review by Дьяконов А. Н. and Редько А. В. citing the same «Материалы симпозиума Fuji, Москва, 1988» plus SMPTE J. So they are ONE source in two renderings: where they overlap numerically the agreement is a transcription check, not a second measurement. GROUNDS ONE STORED NUMBER, rms_granularity 4.0: 1989 Table 1, journal p70, verified against the page image at 300 dpi. Same table prints the response at 30 mm^-1 as 0.60 for this stock -- SEE THE PROFILE COMMENT, it conflicts with both Honjo's 42 c/mm and the 1990 article's own Fig. 3, and nothing was changed on account of it. ⚠ THE CODE RULE, which is the finding that removed FUJI_F125_8630 from this file: 1989 p70 prints Fuji's four-digit scheme in words -- first digit 8 = colour negative, SECOND DIGIT = GAUGE (5 = 35 mm, 6 = 16 mm), last two digits = the film. Applied consistently in 1989 Table 2 (p70) and 1990 Tables 1 and 3 (pp 56, 59) across all five F-series stocks, and matched by Fuji's own Super F-125 sheet ("35mm Type 8532 / 16mm Type 8632"). 8530 and 8630 are therefore one emulsion in two gauges, and 8630 was deleted rather than maintained. CORROBORATED, NOT ADOPTED: exposure index 125 at 3200 K and 80 in daylight with Fuji LBA-12 / Wratten 85 (1990 Table 2, p56) -- identical to the Super F-125 sheet, so the stored EI 125 now has two documents behind it; edge marking No. 30 and yellow packaging (1990 Table 3, p59); the emulsion is thinner by "более чем на 10 %" than type A and lays its GREEN record down in three sub-layers against two for blue and red (1990 Fig. 5 and text, p57), which the stored `layer_stack` can be checked against. ⚠ AVAILABLE AND DELIBERATELY UNHARVESTED, each with its reason, so a later pass does not re-litigate them: (1) 1990 Fig. 4 p57 is a full measured sigma(D) curve, 48 um aperture -- not stored because F-125 and F-64/F-64D converge inside the line width above D ~ 0.9, which is where the only validating anchor sits. It does show sigma PEAKING at D ~ 0.47 and falling to D 1.3 on all six emulsions, against the legacy sqrt(D) law. (2) 1990 Fig. 1 p57 plots this emulsion's three characteristic curves -- not usable for gamma because Fuji drew F-125 and type A SUPERIMPOSED at matched speed ("совпадающих по светочувствительности"), so the three visible tracks each carry two films, and because the abscissa has NO numeric labels at all, only a 0.5-decade scale bar. Fig. 2 (F-64/F-125/F-250, nine curves) has the same abscissa limitation but not the superposition, and is the better candidate if gammas are ever wanted. (3) 1990 Fig. 6 p58 plots this emulsion's spectral sensitivity against type A, 400-700 nm -- not stored because the plate states NO density criterion, so `SpectralSensitivity.criterion` would have to be invented. (4) 1989 Fig. 6 p70 is a spectral density curve for F-500 (type 8514), an emulsion this file does not hold. ⚠ ONE TRANSCRIPTION FAULT IN THE 1989 ARTICLE, recorded so it cannot be copied: its Table 2 prints the F-500 35 mm code as 8415 and the 16 mm as 8525, where Table 1 of the same article, the 1990 Fuji-authored table and the footnote "Код кинопленки F-500 не изменился" all give 8514/8524. The F-125 row is identical in every table. Prefer the 1990 rendering for codes
        // Data source: 本庄 知 [Honjo, Satoru], «動画と静止画 / Moving and Still Images», 日本写真学会誌 [Journal of the Society of Photographic Science and Technology of Japan] 52(6), 1989, pp. 509-516 -- PDF/PROFILES/FUJI/52_509.pdf. Author affiliation printed on p509: 富士写真フイルム(株)足柄研究所 [Fuji Photo Film Co., Ltd., Ashigara Research Laboratories], so this is a MANUFACTURER-AUTHORED figure published in a learned journal, not a third-party estimate -- but it is a REVIEW ESSAY on the psychology of viewing moving versus still images, NOT a datasheet, and it must not be cited as one. PROVENANCE CLASS: manufacturer-authored worked example. GROUNDS EXACTLY ONE STORED NUMBER, f50_g: Table 1.2 (printed p511), headed «Motion Picture Master Nega Film => Dupe Nega Film», footnotes the master negative as '* Fujicolor Negative Film F-125 (8530)', the intermediate as '** Fujicolor Intermediate Film FCI (8213)' and the frame as '*** Frame size: 16.5 mm x 22 mm'; its single data row reads Vis. | nu_50 42 c/mm | pel size 11.9 um | 2.56e6 pels/frame, with the dupe negative at nu_50 26 c/mm. Fig. 3 (printed p512), «Deterioration of MTF of motion picture film images during duplication», plots five curves -- 1 Optical Printing, 2 Shooting Nega Film (E.I. 125), 3 Intermediate Film, 4 Dupe Process (1x3x1x3), 5 Dupe Nega Film (2x4) -- against 0-70 cycles/mm, annotated 'Film MTFs are based on Visual Density'. Curve 2 is this film: it overshoots to ~113 % near 8-10 c/mm and crosses 50 % at the tabulated 42 c/mm. The body text states the film is represented by the MAGENTA image (G density) because that layer occupies the centre of the stack and is visually the most important, which is why 42 c/mm is assigned to f50_g and not to a channel average. ALSO CORROBORATES the stored EI 125 (Fig. 3's own curve label) and the Academy 35 mm frame. ⚠ NOT GROUNDED BY THIS SOURCE, i.e. still estimate-grade: the characteristic curves and their gammas, dmin ladder, clump sizes, halation, dye_matrix, base_tint, reciprocity, and the f50 values for the RED and BLUE layers (37.7 / 45.2 are the previous estimated layer ratio rescaled to the measured green, an explicit interpolation). ⚠ ONE FIGURE FROM THIS PAPER IS DELIBERATELY NOT STORED ANYWHERE YET, because no field exists for it: §4 (printed p515) states that the eye's integration time of about 0.2 s at 24 frames/s means a viewer of MOVING film integrates roughly 5 frames, so perceived granularity falls by 1/sqrt(5) relative to a frozen frame. For a plugin that renders motion this is a real perceptual law and the only statement of it in the corpus. Recorded in DIGITIZATION_QUEUE.md rather than silently folded into a grain number. ⚠ ADJACENT DATA, NOT BACK-APPLIED: Table 1.1 of the same paper gives Fujichrome RPD (ISO 100) 120 roll film nu_50 = 21 / 28 / 41 c/mm for R / G / B at a 55 x 55 mm frame. That stock is not in this database, and the R<G<B ordering there is per-layer whereas the 42 c/mm above is a visual-density composite -- the two are different measurements and must not be mixed
